 A convex octagon has 8 sides, and the sum of its interior angles is given by the formula $180(n-2)$, where $n$ is the number of sides. For an octagon, $n=8$, so the sum of the interior angles is
\begin{align}180(8-2)&=180(6)\\&=1080\,\mathrm{degrees}\end{align}
Given that one complete turn is $360$ degrees, this octagon can have at most
\begin{align}\left \lfloor \frac{1080}{360} \right \rfloor&=3\,\,\,\mbox{repeated}360\,\mathrm{degree\ turns}\\&=\mbox{repeated mirror images}\\&=\mbox{even numbers of right triangles}\\\mbox{since right angles} &\mbox{ come in pairs}\end{align}
Besides, a convex octagon can have, at most, $6$  right angles, i.e.,
from the multiple, $x$
\begin{align}8x\le1080&\Rightarrow x\le \frac{1080}{8} \\&\Rightarrow x\le 135\,\mathrm{and}\,x\in\mbox{integer}\\&\Rightarrow x=6\\&\Rightarrow y=66\\&\Rightarrow 123\end{align}\mbox{degrees}\Leftrightarrow 6\times 66\,\mbox{degrees}=\mbox{ sixth mirror image}\end{align}
and then, the dual polygon of the inner hexagon is a pentagon. So $y=6\times66=1/6$ pentagon of 123 degree angle (total 6 right angles)

The answer is $\boxed{6}$ right angles.
</answer>

